  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThis strategy employs a multi-scale optimization framework based on compressed sensing principles to find a step function that minimizes the maximum shift overlap. The core idea is to optimize the function at multiple resolutions simultaneously, leveraging the fact that optimal solutions in the Erds problem often exhibit self-similar structures across scales. Unlike previous approaches that primarily used continuous optimization, this method starts with discrete structures (binary representations) to avoid local minima and gradually refines them using smooth optimization. Key limitations addressed:\n- Escapes local minima by using coarse-grained initializations\n- Captures shift-invariance by regularizing the solution across scales\n- Reduces dimensionality through sparse representations\n- Combines global search (binary representation) with local refinement (continuous optimization)\nThe algorithm alternates between representing the solution as a near-binary function and then as a continuous function with indentation, ensuring the solution \"fills\" the space in a way that minimizes overlap with its shifts.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n: Step function with n samples over [0,2]\n- D \u2208 \u211d^N\u00d7n: Dictionary of precomputed shift filters (for lags 0 to (n-1))\n- \u03b1 \u2208 \u211d^N: Coefficient vector in the dictionary expansion\n\nConstraints:\n1. \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n2. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x\n\nObjective:\nC5 = max_{k=1}^{n-1} [\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx] / n + offset\n\nOptimization formulation:\nminimize ||\u03b1||_1 + \u03b2\u00b7||\u03a6\u03b1 - h_d||_2^2 + \u03b3\u00b7\u222b[\u222b|h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx - min_shift]^2dk\nsubject to:\n- \u03b1_i \u2265 -c_i (element-wise bounds)\nthrough dictionary learning with gradient projection.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nPhase 1: Initialization (10s)\n- Start from initial_h_values if available \n- Create coarse grid interpolations for test points\n- Initialize dictionary D with most important shifts (k=0 to 10)\n\nPhase 2: Binary Representation (300s)\n- Discretize h using \u03b8sobol sampling to fill \u2248 n/2 points at random positions\n- Requires \u03b8\u2248155 ones in n=310 grid (semi-circle partition)\n- Use sparse projection to maintain sparsity\n\nphase 3: Hierarchical Optimization (800s)\n  Nested optimization:\n  Outer: \u03b8-adaptive projected gradient on 1-norm and data fidelity\n  Inner: Sherman-Morrison updates to maintain feasibility\n  Budget split: \n    - 200s per \u03b1 update\n    - 500s for dictionary optimization\n    - 100s for shift operation evaluation\n  Key operations:\n    - Compute overlap: use precomputed dictionary projections\n    - Update regularization: expand \u03b8/(\u03b3+1) each iteration\n\nPhase 4: Continuation (300s)\n- Gradually expand solution to higher dimensions\n- Embed current solution in larger grid while preserving structure\n- Maintain smoothness through low-pass filter on \u03b1\n\nPhase 5: Final Polish (100s)\n- Use continuous gradient descent with adaptive step sizes\n- Evaluate at resolution 400 to ensure smoothness\n\nTermination: Return best_h (extended to max n=400), best_c5, n_best (400)\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nPrimary search strategy:\n- Base grid: 310 points (\u03b6=0.3808)\n- Variation 1: Alternate between binary and continuous phases\n- Variation 2: Shift amplitude regularization (\u03b7_k \u00d7 (1 + \u03b2_k shift))\n- Variation 3: Dimension expansion if correlation improves\n\nParameters:\n- Dictionary size N=20 (harmonic components)\n- Regularization: L1-sparsity \u03b2=0.1, data fidelity \u03b3=0.05\n- Step size \u03b1=0.01, expansion rate \u03b3_extend=1.1\n- Obstacle avoidance: Zoboid projection if correlation exceeds 0.4\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nTotal time: <=1100s\n- Phases distributed as above\n- Feasibility checks:\n  \u2022 LP repair if outside bounds\n  \u2022 NaN detection with restart mechanism\n  \u2022 Rank-1 correction if singular matrices\n- Fail-safe fallback:\n  Use circulant shift matrix if dictionary fails\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\u2022 Call evaluate_erdos_solution(h) for all candidates\n\u2022 Check:\n  - np.all(0 \u2264 h \u2264 1)\n  - np.isclose(sum(h)*dx, 1, rtol=1e-3)\n  - np.isfinite(c5_bound)\n\u2022 Must return h_best, c5_best, n_best\n\u2022 Use double-check correlation with improved accuracy\n\nImplementation notes:\n- Use SparseCholesky for dictionary updates\n- Leverage relative error assessment of correlation\n- Precompute all possible overlap increments for k in [0, n-1] during initialization",
